              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 65,
                "start": 65
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "Petitioners argue that expert\u2011witness fees were never part of \u201cfull costs\u201d in copyright cases from 1831 to 1976."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioners",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0003-0003"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 67,
                "start": 67
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioners, they contend that \u201cfull costs\u201d is a term of art that does not automatically include non\u2011scheduled fees such as expert\u2011witness fees."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioners",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0003-0002",
                "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0003-0003"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 67,
                "start": 66
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioners, they note that Congress in 1976 enacted statutes expressly authorizing expert\u2011witness fees, but the Supreme Court has consistently treated \u201ccosts\u201d as a term of art that Congress can override only by explicit statement."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioners",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0003-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 68,
                "start": 68
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioners, they assert that non\u2011scheduled fees have never been included in \u201cfull costs,\u201d and that the Court should not reinterpret the term to expand cost awards."
        }
      ],
